WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.139
Routing begins with capability.

00:00:02.139 --> 00:00:11.666
A request should reach a node that has the requested model loaded, supports the required context and features, and has enough available memory.

00:00:11.666 --> 00:00:22.360
Within that eligible group, the router may choose the least-loaded instance, follow a weighted policy, or preserve session affinity when a workflow depends on local state.

00:00:22.360 --> 00:00:29.165
Health checks must remove failed or degraded nodes quickly without sending traffic into a restart loop.

00:00:29.165 --> 00:00:31.304
Concurrency describes simultaneous work.

00:00:31.304 --> 00:00:38.887
Each active sequence consumes cache and compute, so accepting more requests can reduce per-user speed or exhaust memory.

00:00:38.887 --> 00:00:46.469
Queueing protects the server by holding work until capacity is available, but queue delay contributes directly to latency.

00:00:46.469 --> 00:00:50.553
Establish maximum queue depth, request timeouts, and admission controls.

00:00:50.553 --> 00:00:58.135
When the service is saturated, a clear busy response is safer than allowing every node to become unresponsive.

00:00:58.135 --> 00:01:02.996
Batching groups compatible work to improve accelerator utilization and total throughput.

00:01:02.996 --> 00:01:09.801
The tradeoff is that a request may wait for a batch and share compute with others.

00:01:09.801 --> 00:01:18.162
Interactive chat usually prioritizes low time to first token, while offline processing may accept delay in exchange for higher throughput.

00:01:18.162 --> 00:01:23.801
Separate service classes or queues when one policy cannot serve both workloads fairly.

00:01:23.801 --> 00:01:27.884
Failover is more than pointing at another IP address.

00:01:27.884 --> 00:01:33.522
The alternate node must have the correct model version, configuration, capacity, and dependencies.

00:01:33.522 --> 00:01:38.383
Test what happens to in-flight requests and whether clients retry safely.

00:01:38.383 --> 00:01:43.633
Track arrival rate, service time, queue time, rejection rate, and resource saturation.

00:01:43.633 --> 00:01:46.938
Scale only after identifying the constrained stage.

00:01:46.938 --> 00:01:56.854
Adding a GPU worker will not fix a slow shared storage service, an overloaded router, or a network path that cannot carry the traffic.
